sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: the step "Save the new images" of the seed refresh, stage 6 of the command development pipeline (docs/reference/pipeline/6-task-image-seed-refresh.mmd), as a kind of its own so that it runs on any running task VM with a nested fleet. The refresh calls the same workflow as a child once it starts its own refresh VM.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · stage 6 saves the ready copies
  %% kind: task_vm.ready_copies
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>over ssh
  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>task VM · its nested fleet
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the operator command `task-vms:save-ready-copies VM` on the Gateway; there is no orbit CLI command
  %%> - later, the seed refresh of stage 6, as a child workflow in its refresh VM
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the task VM is `ready`, its guest Node is active with a recorded host key
  %%> - the nested fleet runs in it: `nested-gateway`, `nested-app-dev` and `nested-app-prod`, on the network `oe-nested`
  %%> - no other task holds `task_vm:<id>`; a repeated admission returns the open task
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the task records the VM, its guest Node, its host key pin and the names of the nested VMs
  %%> - the proof Swamp Club key goes to the vault as a copy; the intent holds only its name
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-ready-copies
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); every step is one `incus` command over official `@swamp/ssh` to the VM, with literal argv
  SW->>VM: Remove the operator key<br/>incus exec · sqlite3, perl
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - sets the WireGuard key of the nested Gateway's `operator` Node record to null in the database its `.env` names, and takes the operator peer (10.45.0.10) out of the WireGuard files of the Gateway hub
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the key belongs to the VM that made the copies; a task VM that starts them makes its own (stage 2), so the old key works nowhere else
  SW->>VM: Delete and scan, trim<br/>incus exec · grep, fstrim
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - deletes the proof Swamp Club key (`/home/orbit/.config/swamp/api-key`) from the nested Gateway, then searches every file of all three guests for it by value, with the key on standard input
  %%> - runs `fstrim` so the freed blocks of the deleted key are discarded from the disk image that every task VM clones
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the seed is cloned into every task VM of the host; a key in it would reach all of them
  SW->>VM: Order the state mounts<br/>_netdev on the units
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - sets `_netdev` on the NFS mount of the checkout and on the three bind-mount units of the state in the Gateway and app-dev guests, then reloads systemd; it edits the unit files only, never the links in `multi-user.target.wants`
  %%> Why:
  %%> - as local mounts they close an ordering cycle with the NFS mount of the checkout at boot (`local-fs.target`, the NFS mount, `network.target`, `ufw.service`), and systemd drops them; as network mounts they come up after the NFS mount, so the apply step need not start them by name
  SW->>VM: Stop the nested VMs<br/>incus stop
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one clean stop of the three guests, so a copy holds finished file systems
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - the Gateway's Horizon unit is ordered after `valkey-server.service`: Horizon drains through its queue and never gets SIGKILL, so when both stopped together the Gateway guest hung at shutdown (op 11691)
  SW->>VM: Copy the guests<br/>incus copy --instance-only
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - deletes the earlier `fleet-ready-*` copies, then copies each guest to `fleet-ready-gateway`, `fleet-ready-app-dev` and `fleet-ready-app-prod`, without the `base` snapshot of the earlier fleet
  %%> - removes the NIC from each copy
  %%> Self-contained:
  %%> - a copy keeps its `.env`, `storage`, `bootstrap/cache` and database on its own disk and holds no checkout; every task VM mounts its own over NFS (stage 2)
  %%> - its NFS mount names the export path that every task VM has, `/srv/checkout`, never a workspace path, so one set serves a task VM of any Project (decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review, 2026-11-06)
  SW->>VM: Record the network<br/>incus config set
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stores on each copy `user.ready.subnet`, `user.ready.hwaddr` and `user.ready.ipv4`, its role and the time; a task VM creates the network from the subnet
  %%> - writes `user.ready.complete` last, on all three: a set without it is half made
  %%> - writes `user.ready.format` with it, the format of the copies; a provision starts a fleet only from copies of the current format
  SW->>VM: Boot-check the copies<br/>incus copy, start, exec
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts a throwaway copy of each ready copy, waits for its agent, and checks it: no Club key, no file of a checkout but a log, the state on its own disk, no operator key in the record or on the hub
  %%> - the ready copies themselves are never started
  SW->>VM: Remove the check copies<br/>incus delete
  %%> Why:
  %%> - runs after success, failure and cancellation
  SW->>SW: Check the ready copies<br/>assert
  %%> End check:
  %%> - a native `assert`: the NFS mount and every state unit of a stored copy have `_netdev`, no operator key in the record, all three copies stateful and without a NIC
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>ready copies saved
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Gateway records the task as succeeded; the copies are in the VM's seed disk, ready to become the host's seed
  else a step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> On the VM:
    %%> - a failure before the copy step leaves the earlier copies; the nested VMs may stay stopped, so start them before the next run
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the task fails with the failed step named; the next run starts again
  end
